-- title:
--   Elements $p$ of the hereditary subalgebra of $(a-\delta)_+$ satisfy $\delta\,p^*p\le p^*ap$
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a C*-algebra, possibly non-unital, with its usual order: $x\le y$ means that $y-x$ is positive, i.e. self-adjoint with spectrum in $[0,\infty)$. For $h\in A$ let
--   $$\operatorname{Her}(h)=\overline{\{\,h\,x\,h^*:\ x\in A\,\}}$$
--   be the norm closure of the $*$-subalgebra of all sandwiches $hxh^*$; for positive $h$ this is the hereditary C*-subalgebra $\overline{hAh}$ generated by $h$. For a positive $a\in A$ and a real number $\delta\ge0$, let $(a-\delta)_+=f_\delta(a)$ be defined by continuous functional calculus with $f_\delta(t)=\max(t-\delta,0)$.
--
--   **Theorem.** Let $a\in A$ be positive, let $\delta\ge0$, and let $p\in\operatorname{Her}\bigl((a-\delta)_+\bigr)$. Then
--   $$\delta\,p^*p\;\le\;p^*\,a\,p .$$
--
--   In words: on the hereditary subalgebra cut out by the part of the spectrum of $a$ above the level $\delta$, the element $a$ dominates $\delta$ in the sense of quadratic forms. The inequality is a basic tool for turning membership in such a hereditary subalgebra into quantitative information. Conjugating by an arbitrary $w$ gives $\delta\,\|pw\|^2\le\|(pw)^*a(pw)\|$, which bounds the size of comparison elements, and applying a $*$-homomorphism $\pi$ shows that $\pi(p^*ap)=0$ forces $\pi(p)=0$ when $\delta>0$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The order on $A$ is the C*-algebraic spectral order (`CStarAlgebra.spectralOrder` together with `CStarAlgebra.spectralOrderedRing`), installed as local instances. $\operatorname{Her}(h)$ is `Her h`, the topological closure of the star-subalgebra `sandwichAlgebra h` whose carrier is the range of $x\mapsto hxh^*$; $(a-\delta)_+$ is `cut a δ`, defined with the non-unital continuous functional calculus `cfcₙ`; and $\delta\,p^*p$ is the real scalar multiple `δ • (star p * p)`.
